# npm on Windows always shells out to cmd.exe for package.json scripts by
# default (a documented npm quirk based on process.platform) — true even
# when npm is invoked from WSL bash, because no native Linux Node/npm is
# installed in WSL here; `which npm` resolves to the Windows npm mounted at
# /mnt/c/Program Files/nodejs, so it's still a Windows process underneath.
# contracts/midnight's `compile` script uses bash syntax (for loops, ||,
# grep), which cmd.exe cannot parse at all ("f was unexpected at this
# time."). Forcing bash here fixes that syntax error and makes the
# script's own PATH-guard (see contracts/midnight/package.json's `compile`
# script) run and report cleanly instead of crashing with a cmd.exe parse
# error, from both Git Bash and WSL.
#
# Lives at the WORKSPACE ROOT, not inside contracts/midnight, because npm
# workspaces only read the root .npmrc — a package-level .npmrc is silently
# ignored ("npm warn config ignoring workspace config at .../.npmrc") once
# that package is part of a workspace (found 2026-07-30 converting this
# repo to a workspace — see package.json).
#
# Known residual limitation: this does NOT make `npm run compile` fully
# self-sufficient end-to-end on Windows. The bash spawned by Windows npm
# resolves via Windows PATH (Git Bash/MSYS), which cannot see WSL's
# ~/.local/bin/compact inside WSL — so the guard will correctly, loudly fail
# with instructions rather than silently passing, but `npm run compile`
# itself still cannot succeed without a native Linux Node/npm inside WSL
# (a system-level install, out of scope here). Until then, the real
# working local test workflow is two separate steps: (1) compile via WSL
# directly — `wsl.exe -e bash -lc "cd <path> && for f in bonding_curve
# eligibility_gate treasury creator_escrow vesting lp_escrow cto_governance
# staking_pool; do compact compile --skip-zk \"$f.compact\" \"compiled/$f\";
# done"` — then (2) `npx vitest run` from Git Bash/native, which needs only
# the already-compiled JS output and @midnight-ntwrk/compact-runtime, not
# the `compact` CLI itself. CI is unaffected either way — the GitHub
# Actions runner is real Linux with the real Compact CLI installed, so
# `npm run compile` there needs none of this.
script-shell=bash
